layout: layouts/tool.njk
toolSlug: mean-median-mode-calculator
title: "Mean, Median, Mode & Standard Deviation Calculator"
description: "Paste a list of numbers to get the mean, median, mode, range, quartiles, variance and population and sample standard deviation, with the working."
permalink: /tools/mean-median-mode-calculator/
h1: "Mean, Median, Mode & Standard Deviation Calculator"
extraCss: "/tools/mean-median-mode-calculator/style.css"
quickAnswer: "The mean is the sum divided by the count, the median is the middle value once the numbers are sorted, and the mode is the most frequent value. For 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9 the mean is 40 ÷ 8 = 5, the median is (4 + 5) ÷ 2 = 4.5 and the mode is 4. The population standard deviation is 2, and the sample standard deviation (dividing by n − 1) is 2.1381."
formula:
  title: "Statistics Formulas"
  expression: "Mean x̄ = Σx ÷ n   ·   Population SD σ = √(Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ n)   ·   Sample SD s = √(Σ(x − x̄)² ÷ (n − 1))"
  explanation: "The median is the middle value of the sorted list, or the average of the two middle values when the count is even. The mode is the value that appears most often; if several values tie for the highest count they are all modes, and if every value appears equally often there is no mode. Quartiles use the median-of-halves method: Q1 is the median of the lower half and Q3 the median of the upper half, leaving out the overall median when the count is odd. Variance is the average squared distance from the mean, and standard deviation is its square root."
  variables:
    - name: "Population (σ, divide by n)"
      description: "Use when your numbers are the whole group you care about, such as every test score in a class."
    - name: "Sample (s, divide by n − 1)"
      description: "Use when your numbers are a sample from a bigger group and you want to estimate that group's spread (Bessel's correction)."
    - name: "IQR"
      description: "Interquartile range, Q3 − Q1: the spread of the middle half of the data."
workedExamples:
  - title: "2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9"
    inputs:
      - "8 values"
    steps:
      - "Mean: (2 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 5 + 5 + 7 + 9) ÷ 8 = 40 ÷ 8 = 5"
      - "Median: 8 values, so average the 4th and 5th: (4 + 5) ÷ 2 = 4.5"
      - "Mode: 4 appears 3 times, more than any other value"
      - "Squared deviations: 9, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 4, 16; sum = 32"
      - "Population SD: √(32 ÷ 8) = √4 = 2; sample SD: √(32 ÷ 7) = 2.1381"
    result: "Mean 5, median 4.5, mode 4, σ = 2, s = 2.1381"
    link: "/tools/mean-median-mode-calculator/?data=2%2C4%2C4%2C4%2C5%2C5%2C7%2C9#tool"
  - title: "1, 2, 3 (no mode)"
    inputs:
      - "3 values"
    steps:
      - "Mean: 6 ÷ 3 = 2; median: the middle value, 2"
      - "Each value appears once, so there is no mode"
      - "Population SD: √(2 ÷ 3) = 0.8165; sample SD: √(2 ÷ 2) = 1"
    result: "Mean 2, median 2, no mode"
    link: "/tools/mean-median-mode-calculator/?data=1%2C2%2C3#tool"
  - title: "Quartiles of 1, 3, 5, 7, 9"
    inputs:
      - "5 values (odd count)"
    steps:
      - "Median: 5 (the 3rd value)"
      - "Lower half without the median: 1, 3, so Q1 = 2"
      - "Upper half without the median: 7, 9, so Q3 = 8"
    result: "Q1 = 2, Q3 = 8, IQR = 6"
    link: "/tools/mean-median-mode-calculator/?data=1%2C3%2C5%2C7%2C9#tool"
commonMistakes:
  - title: "Forgetting to sort before finding the median"
    description: "The median is the middle of the <em>sorted</em> list. The middle of 9, 2, 5 as typed is 2, but the median is 5."
  - title: "Using the population formula for a sample"
    description: "Dividing by n underestimates the spread when your data is a sample from a larger group. Use the sample standard deviation (n − 1) for surveys, experiments and measurements of a subset."
  - title: "Expecting one mode"
    description: "Data can have one mode, several modes, or none. 1, 1, 2, 2, 3 has two modes (1 and 2); 1, 2, 3 has none."
  - title: "Comparing quartiles from different tools"
    description: "There are several accepted ways to calculate quartiles, and they can give slightly different answers for the same data. Spreadsheets often interpolate (Excel's QUARTILE.INC); this calculator uses the median-of-halves method taught in many courses."
  - title: "Typing commas as decimal points"
    description: "Commas separate values here, so 2,5 is read as two numbers, 2 and 5. Use a dot for decimals: 2.5."
faqs:
  - q: "How do you find the mean, median and mode?"
    a: "Mean: add all values and divide by how many there are. Median: sort the values and take the middle one, or the average of the two middle ones. Mode: the value that appears most often."
  - q: "What is the difference between population and sample standard deviation?"
    a: "Population standard deviation divides the sum of squared deviations by n and describes a complete group. Sample standard deviation divides by n − 1 and estimates the spread of a larger population from a sample, so it is slightly larger."
  - q: "How do I calculate standard deviation by hand?"
    a: "Find the mean, subtract it from each value and square the result, add those squares, divide by n (population) or n − 1 (sample), then take the square root. The calculator shows each of these steps."
  - q: "What if there is no mode?"
    a: "If every value appears the same number of times, no value is more common than the others and the data has no mode. 1, 2, 3 has no mode."
  - q: "Can a data set have more than one mode?"
    a: "Yes. If two or more values tie for the highest count, they are all modes. 1, 1, 2, 2, 3 is bimodal with modes 1 and 2."
  - q: "How are the quartiles calculated?"
    a: "This calculator uses the median-of-halves method: split the sorted data into a lower and upper half (leaving out the median when the count is odd) and take the median of each half as Q1 and Q3."
  - q: "How should I enter my numbers?"
    a: "Separate numbers with commas, spaces, semicolons or new lines, and use a dot for decimals. You can paste a column straight from a spreadsheet."
  - q: "Is the median or the mean better?"
    a: "The mean uses every value but is pulled by outliers; the median is the middle value and is not affected by a few extreme numbers. For skewed data such as incomes or house prices, the median is usually more representative."
sources:
