    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Mean, Median or Mode: Which Average to Use</h2>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table class="table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">When to use the mean, median and mode</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Measure</th><th scope="col">What it tells you</th><th scope="col">Best for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Mean</td><td>The balance point of all values</td><td>Symmetric data without extreme outliers, such as heights or test scores</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Median</td><td>The middle value; half are above, half below</td><td>Skewed data or data with outliers, such as incomes and house prices</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mode</td><td>The most common value</td><td>Categories and repeated values, such as shoe sizes or survey answers</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>An outlier can move the mean a long way while leaving the median almost unchanged. For the values 30, 32, 35, 38 and 40, the mean and median are both 35. Replace 40 with 400 and the median stays 35, but the mean jumps to 107. That is why official income statistics usually report the median.</p>

      <h2>Reading the Standard Deviation</h2>
      <p>Standard deviation measures how far values typically sit from the mean, in the same units as the data. A small standard deviation means the values cluster tightly; a large one means they are spread out. For data shaped like a bell curve, about 68% of values fall within one standard deviation of the mean and about 95% within two.</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Variance</strong> is the standard deviation squared. It is used in further calculations but is in squared units, so it is harder to interpret directly.</li>
        <li><strong>Range</strong> (max − min) is quick but depends only on the two most extreme values.</li>
        <li><strong>Interquartile range</strong> (Q3 − Q1) covers the middle half of the data and ignores outliers. A common rule flags values more than 1.5 × IQR below Q1 or above Q3 as possible outliers.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>In spreadsheets, STDEV.P and VAR.P match the population figures here, and STDEV.S and VAR.S match the sample figures. All calculations run in your browser, so you can paste private data safely.</p>
    </section>
